{
  "id": "BC-006-multi-interpreter",
  "class_name": "TestMultiInterpreterDashE",
  "description": "Inline source for non-Python interpreters (node, ruby, perl, pypy) with literal protected paths embedded in the source. DEF-832 (2026-09-16): a program operand is the one bash word it is -- adjacent quoted and bare segments concatenated with the shell's quote removal -- so a path quoted inside a single-quoted program (spliced or naively) is read on the interpreter and POSIX shell openers; the three attempts a10-a12 are pinned by tests/test_write_guard.py::TestShellOutReader.Same shape as the python -c capture; extending to peer interpreters is the most direct closure of the documented 'interpreter execution' bypass class. DEF-704 (2026-09-07) anchored the four openers on the command position; a6-a8 sit BEHIND `&&`, `sudo` and a quoted path with a space, so an anchor that only ever saw offset 0 cannot pass this class. The PowerShell tool has its twin in BC-052 (DEF-712, 2026-09-08): the same interpreters through `_PS_CMD_POS`-anchored openers, the body sliced from a same-length raw twin. DEF-813 (2026-09-15) added the THREE-argument perl open (a9): the mode and the path in separate strings, the spelling perl's own documentation recommends and a lexical filehandle forces, which the two-argument arm never read -- `_PERL_OPEN3_RE`, in the one inner table both shells share.",
  "in_scope": true,
  "documented_in": "tests/test_write_guard.py::TestMultiInterpreterDashE",
